No worries.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:05.069
So how we think about
data science as a journey.

00:01:05.069 --> 00:01:08.820
It's a very iterative process,
as I'm sure you're all aware.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:12.330
It starts with a hypothesis.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:16.690
There's development and data
involved in that development.

00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:18.180
There is collaboration
with others,

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:20.080
with other teams,
other stakeholders.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:23.040
And then at the end is
some type of deliverable,

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:27.570
whether that's automation,
or handoff to the FDA.

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:29.850
And so in pharma,
oftentimes what

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:33.240
this looks like is something
like early stage research where

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:36.690
you're developing that
hypothesis that might evolve

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:41.730
into a full blown
experimental design and trial

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:45.600
and then into something
like a report or submission.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:50.410
It might look more like
manufacturing or marketing.

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:53.010
And those are also key
parts of the pharma journey,

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:56.250
just determining some
type of objective.

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:59.970
And then iterating to decide if that
was the right objective, maybe

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:03.007
coming up with models and
doing production scoring.

00:02:03.007 --> 00:02:04.590
And so there's a lot
of different ways

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:07.050
this kind of journey
can be realized

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:08.880
in the pharmaceutical space.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:10.419
But what I want
to show you today

00:02:10.419 --> 00:02:12.527
is how we're building
tools to help

00:02:12.527 --> 00:02:14.610
and where those tools fit
into the different parts

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:15.720
of this journey.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:19.200
So on the top, you
have some major updates

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.870
we've released in the
last year to RStudios,

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:23.910
professional products.

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:27.570
So on the development side,
we've done a lot of work

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:32.070
to ensure that RStudio scales
as your data in the richness

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:35.250
of that data grows by allowing
you to tap into things

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:37.740
like the Cloud in
the middle part

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:39.870
there. Where you're thinking
about collaboration

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:41.260
and iteration,

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:43.890
we've done a lot of work on a
tool called Rstudio Connect

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:47.070
to help you dynamically
share content.

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:50.070
And then when it comes to
hardening in automation,

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:53.940
we've done a lot of work on a
project called package manager

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:57.120
to ensure that whether
you're doing the analysis

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:00.540
in R or Python, whether it's
using CRAN or Bioconductor,

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:04.080
you have the correct packages
and dependencies that you need

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:05.730
to be really certain
that your work's

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:07.770
reproducible and reliable.

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:09.480
So those are things in blue.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:13.110
Those are kind of all available
today as part of RStudio

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:16.530
Team, which is that
professional platform for doing

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:19.830
data science that you'll hear
a little bit about this week.

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:21.728
But on the bottom
