00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:33.230
And so if you're using
RStudio package manager,

00:06:33.230 --> 00:06:38.040
you can actually go ahead and
keep those two things in sync.

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:40.010
So that as your
working with packages,

00:06:40.010 --> 00:06:41.930
and small packages
from CRAN, you'll

00:06:41.930 --> 00:06:44.200
be guaranteed to get
the right results.

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:47.330
I'm showing you today kind of
an internal version of this,

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:49.760
but you can actually
go to package manager

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:51.860
dot RStudio,
dot com, which is

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:57.140
a free version of the package
manager service and the Bioconductor

00:06:57.140 --> 00:06:59.690
offering is available
there as well.

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:04.400
So if you want to source
Bioconductor packages, as well as

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:06.470
kind of a frozen
version of CRAN that

00:07:06.470 --> 00:07:08.370
will match the
Bioconnector release.

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:12.120
And that's available
to you today.

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:14.620
So I know we're going to have
in rapid fire succession,

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:16.620
but the last thing I
wanted to talk a little bit

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:19.650
about with some of those
changes in the open source

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:24.210
ecosystem around R Markdown
and some of the packages

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:25.860
that work with R
Markdown to create

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:29.550
these beautiful documents that
you might be using internally

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:31.380
or for things like submissions.

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:33.810
And so I'm going to talk about
two things, an update with

00:07:33.810 --> 00:07:34.660
R Markdown.

00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:37.380
And this package called GT.

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:39.330
And so we'll kind of
combine both of those

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:40.580
into a single demo.

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:42.690
But before we hop
to the IDE, just

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:45.780
to give you a
understanding of gt it's

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:49.050
a package short for
grammar of tables.

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:51.360
So it's very
similar to ggplt2,

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:53.430
which is the grammar of plots.

00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:56.700
And so what GT is
all about is allowing

00:07:56.700 --> 00:08:00.030
you to write really expressive
and flexible code for creating

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:02.850
tables, and then take
that code and use

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:05.700
it to render those tables
wherever you need them.

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:09.570
So the same code can be
used to create tables

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:13.620
that render to RTF things
like Word documents

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:15.900
or to render HTML.

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:18.030
And so that's kind
of the concept.

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:20.820
And I'll show you what
that package looks like

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:24.540
and how you use it
inside of RMarkdown.

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:27.870
So let me switch
over to RStudio IDE.

00:08:29.710 --> 00:08:30.790
This is all open source.

00:08:30.790 --> 00:08:32.820
So you can use the
latest version of RStudio

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:36.120
on your desktop or on a
server with these packages.

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:39.900
And basically the
workflow that you'll see

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:42.120
is similar to what
we're looking at here.

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:43.470
So I have some data.

00:08:43.470 --> 00:08:46.980
This is just kind of raw
data inside of a data frame.

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:50.460
And to turn that data
into a table with gt,

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:53.100
it's very similar to how you
might create a plot with

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:53.730
ggplot2.

00:08:53.730 --> 00:08:56.290
So you start with this gt function.

00:08:56.290 --> 00:09:01.830
And then you add pieces to
get to the final end result.

00:09:01.830 --> 00:09:05.670
So in this case, we're
adding some summary rows.

00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:08.340
We're add a grand
summary of summaries

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:12.840
and then we're telling GT how
to format the different text

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:13.920
cells.

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:17.680
So I'll actually, go ahead and
run this code for you.

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:20.780
And we can take a
look at the output.

00:09:20.780 --> 00:09:25.050
So this is going to
create for us a gt table.

00:09:25.050 --> 00:09:28.190
And by default, that gt table
is going to be in HTML.

00:09:28.190 --> 00:09:32.270
So it's right here inside
of our R Markdown document.

00:09:32.270 --> 00:09:35.780
And you can see kind of
what I had described.

00:09:35.780 --> 00:09:38.040
We start out with this
data that's in a table.

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:40.160
And then we're
adding summary rows.

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:43.910
We're adding a grand summary,
which is this top row here.

00:09:43.910 --> 00:09:45.970
And then we're doing
some formatting.

00:09:45.970 --> 00:09:50.570
You can see things like
footnotes are fully supported,

00:09:50.570 --> 00:09:53.040
different sections of
tables are supported.

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:56.510
You can have custom
formatting, column headers.

00:09:56.510 --> 00:09:59.570
So a lot of flexibility in
creating the table just the way

00:09:59.570 --> 00:10:00.950
you want.

00:10:00.950 --> 00:10:03.170
And that's already
pretty exciting.

00:10:03.170 --> 00:10:07.220
But it gets even better because
with a single line of code,

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:09.800
you can take the same
table that we've created

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:11.930
and save it to a
different output format.

00:10:11.930 --> 00:10:14.030
So if you need this
table to exist inside

00:10:14.030 --> 00:10:17.210
of a Word document, you can
create that RTF version

00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:20.090
of the table that will
then render inside of Word

00:10:20.090 --> 00:10:23.690
or inside of whatever
other formatting

00:10:23.690 --> 00:10:25.580
tool you want to use.

00:10:25.580 --> 00:10:28.800
So that's the really kind
of exciting update to gt.

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:30.347
I mentioned, there
is something else.

00:10:30.347 --> 00:10:32.180
So kind of the last
thing I want to show you

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:34.580
is this update to R Markdown.

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:37.580
And the way it works is
inside of the latest version

00:10:37.580 --> 00:10:38.780
of the IDE.

00:10:38.780 --> 00:10:43.130
There's this new option to
essentially render the document

00:10:43.130 --> 00:10:43.900
as you go.

00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:48.260
And so by clicking this kind of
magic button here, what I get

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:51.770
is an interactive editor
for the R Markdown document.

00:10:51.770 --> 00:10:53.210
So our code is still here.

00:10:53.210 --> 00:10:55.280
You can see our
table is still here.

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:58.610
But but I actually
have the rendering

00:10:58.610 --> 00:11:01.310
of R Markdown in real time.

00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:03.870
And so why would you do this?

00:11:03.870 --> 00:11:05.010
Because it turns out.

00:11:05.010 --> 00:11:06.890
It makes it really
easy to create

00:11:06.890 --> 00:11:08.810
rich and powerful documents.

00:11:08.810 --> 00:11:13.590
And so as an example, I'll go
ahead and insert another table.

00:11:13.590 --> 00:11:17.060
And this table will take a look
at some of the features that

00:11:17.060 --> 00:11:18.110
are available here.

00:11:18.110 --> 00:11:21.200
So as I type markdown,
that markdown is going

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:23.600
to be rendered in real time.

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:27.380
I can even put in
things like emojis.

00:11:27.380 --> 00:11:31.050
So there's full support
for interactive editing,

00:11:31.050 --> 00:11:35.100
and you can even do things
like render Latex in line.

00:11:35.100 --> 00:11:37.110
You can see the
preview shows up.

00:11:37.110 --> 00:11:38.870
And then the output shows up.

00:11:38.870 --> 00:11:42.050
So as you're writing, it
becomes a really powerful way

00:11:42.050 --> 00:11:45.470
to create and see exactly
what you're doing.

00:11:45.470 --> 00:11:47.990
But under the hood, it's
all still R Markdown down.

00:11:47.990 --> 00:11:50.220
So I can always
go back and forth.

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:53.240
If I wanted to, to
the original document.

00:11:53.240 --> 00:11:55.490
That's just a plain text file.

00:11:55.490 --> 00:11:57.700
So we're excited about how
all these things together

00:11:57.700 --> 00:12:02.000
can hopefully help you
in your day to day work,

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:04.640
achieve that in the
science journey.

00:12:04.640 --> 00:12:06.410
And so I'll end things there.

00:12:06.410 --> 00:12:09.210
This is just a little
bit of a preview, a taste,

00:12:09.210 --> 00:12:11.000
a sampling, if you
will, of what's

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:13.325
to come both today at
RStudio conference.

00:12:13.325 --> 00:12:14.950
So I hand things back
over to you, Sam.

00:12:14.950 --> 00:12:16.960
Thanks for having me.

